WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.799
Subsurface scattering means that a portion
of light that hits a translucent material

00:00:10.799 --> 00:00:16.510
does not bounce back from the surface but
penetrates it and scatters many-many times

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:18.099
inside the material.

00:00:18.099 --> 00:00:23.300
Now, if you have a keen eye, you recognize
that there are a lot of materials in real

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.900
life that have subsurface scattering.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:33.040
Many don't know, but our skin is a great example
of that, and so is marble, milk, wax, plant

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.950
leaves, apple and many others.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:40.130
If you would like to hear a bit more about
subsurface scattering, check the second part

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:44.399
of the video that you see recommended in the
corner of this window, or just click it in

00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:46.769
the description box below.

00:00:46.769 --> 00:00:52.300
Subsurface scattering looks unbelievably beautiful,
but at the same time, it is very expensive

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:57.260
because we have to simulate up to thousands
and thousands of scattering events for every

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:58.260
ray of light.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.450
It really takes forever.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:05.890
And if you'd like to tweak your material settings
just a bit because the result is not a 100%

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:11.330
up to your taste, you have to recreate, or
what graphics people like to say, re-render

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:12.390
these images.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:15.410
It's not really a convenient workflow.

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:20.010
This piece of work offers a great solution
where you have to wait a bit longer than you

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:25.881
would wait for one image, but only once, because
it runs a generalized light simulation, and

00:01:25.881 --> 00:01:31.250
after that, whatever changes you apply to
your materials, you will see immediately.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:36.521
You can also paint the reflectance properties
of this material that we call albedos and

00:01:36.521 --> 00:01:51.040
get results with full subsurface scattering
immediately.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.780
Here is another interactive editing workflow
where you get results instantaneously, and

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:01.020
the result with this technique is indistinguishable
from the real deal, which would be re-rendering

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.770
this result image every time some adjustment
is made.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:10.209
With this technique, you can really create
the materials you thought up in a fraction

00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:24.049
of the time of the classical workflow.

00:02:24.049 --> 00:02:25.989
Spectacular work.

00:02:25.989 --> 00:02:29.709
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

